Supply living tissue — blood, oxygen, and metabolism (@med-circulation: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @medicine, #source @med-source-osu, #medicine @med-nerves) { r_51SD84CANGMKYW7X2VNZ6HXTD4
**Read in two passes:** in [Anatomy & Physiology — Oregon State University](../sources/Anatomy-and-Physiology.lingua#r_1WEZWZ5YY6797K4EXVYTBJS25N), first read the complete chapter 18 on blood and the introductory circulation sections of chapters 19–20. Then study the chapter 20 account of vessel structure, exchange, and regulation alongside [Nerves — sensation, muscle activation, and control](Nerves-and-control.lingua#r_08WV4S2F8WMCT7SZX2FHY82YP7). For the broad integrative reading, complete chapters 18–22 and chapter 24; for this question, revisit gas exchange and ATP/metabolism when the explanation needs them.

Arteries/artérias carry blood away from the heart; veins/veias return it. These names describe direction, not a rule that all arterial blood is oxygen-rich. Exchange occurs through microcirculation, including capillaries/capilares. Lymphatic vessels help return tissue fluid.

Perfusion/perfusão is blood delivery through tissue. Oxygen and nutrients support metabolism/metabolismo, including production of ATP used by cells. Blood flow, oxygen delivery, and a cell's ability to use available energy are related but distinct. Injury can also disrupt vessels, cause bleeding, and change local fluid distribution.

This matters in orthopedics because bone, muscle, tendons, and nerves are living tissues with different supply and repair characteristics. Healing cannot be understood from a diagram of fiber direction alone.

**Try:** make a supply-and-return diagram for a patch of shoulder muscle. Separate blood inside a vessel, fluid between cells, and fluid within a joint cavity. Explain why those compartments should not all be called 'water in the shoulder'.

**Continue:** [Nerves — sensation, muscle activation, and control](Nerves-and-control.lingua#r_08WV4S2F8WMCT7SZX2FHY82YP7).

**Return:** [Medicine — understanding the body and its care](../Medicine.lingua#r_6DTDCDP6JQ2AV7S78QPGKM2V65).
